<https://w3id.org/project-chemie-digital/graph/scenes/level7-anova-continuation> {
  ex:l7-scene14 a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l7-next-14-focus ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l7-next-14-item-1, ex:l7-next-14-item-2, ex:l7-next-14-item-3 ; cd:narration "Before calculating variation, fix the question and the design. In this synthetic example, three methods each have six independent preparations, and the response is percent recovery. The null hypothesis says that the three population mean recoveries are equal; the alternative says that at least one differs. These statements concern population means, not whether the observed sample means happen to match. Set the significance level to 0.05 and specify the intended comparison family in advance. Check that preparation, rather than repeated injection, defines the independent unit. Next, we calculate variation within methods."@en ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-14-item-1 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-14-heading ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-14-item-2 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-14-cards ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DefinitionListRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasDefinitionListEntry" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-14-item-3 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-14-takeaway ; cd:communicativeRole cd:StatementRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-scene15 a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l7-next-15-focus ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l7-next-15-item-1, ex:l7-next-15-item-2, ex:l7-next-15-item-3 ; cd:narration "Start with the variation that remains among preparations receiving the same method. Read across each row for method A: subtract its mean of 94 percent from each observation, then square each difference. The displayed squared deviations are one, one, zero, four, four, and zero, which sum to ten. Methods B and C each contribute another ten, giving a within-group sum of squares of thirty squared percentage points. Use each method's own mean, not the overall mean of 95 percent, because this calculation isolates within-method variation. Next, compare the method means with the overall mean."@en ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-15-item-1 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-15-heading ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-15-item-2 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-15-table ; cd:communicativeRole cd:TableRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasTableRow" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-15-item-3 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-15-takeaway ; cd:communicativeRole cd:StatementRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-scene16 a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l7-next-16-focus ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l7-next-16-item-1, ex:l7-next-16-item-2, ex:l7-next-16-item-3 ; cd:narration "Now shift from individual preparations to the three method means. The displayed means are 94, 98, and 93 percent, while the overall mean is 95 percent. Their deviations are minus one, plus three, and minus two percentage points. Squaring gives one, nine, and four. Multiply each by six because each method mean represents six independent preparations. The three contributions are therefore six, fifty-four, and twenty-four, totaling eighty-four squared percentage points. Check the weights in the contribution column: this is weighted variation, not simply the spread of three means. Next, combine between and within variation."@en ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-16-item-1 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-16-heading ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-16-item-2 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-16-table ; cd:communicativeRole cd:TableRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasTableRow" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-16-item-3 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-16-takeaway ; cd:communicativeRole cd:StatementRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-scene17 a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l7-next-17-focus ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l7-next-17-item-1, ex:l7-next-17-item-2, ex:l7-next-17-item-3 ; cd:narration "With both components available, check the calculation against the original observations. Subtract the overall mean of 95 percent from each of the eighteen values, square those deviations, and add them. This direct total is 114 squared percentage points. The three cards show the same accounting from another direction: eighty-four between methods plus thirty within methods equals 114 overall. The total has seventeen degrees of freedom because eighteen observations share one estimated overall mean. Treat this equality as an arithmetic check, not a significance test. Next, divide the components by their degrees of freedom."@en ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-17-item-1 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-17-heading ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-17-item-2 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-17-cards ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DefinitionListRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasDefinitionListEntry" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-17-item-3 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-17-takeaway ; cd:communicativeRole cd:StatementRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-scene18 a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l7-next-18-focus ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l7-next-18-item-1, ex:l7-next-18-item-2, ex:l7-next-18-item-3 ; cd:narration "Read the ANOVA table from left to right, starting with the method row. Its sum of squares is eighty-four and its degrees of freedom are two, so its mean square is forty-two. The residual row has thirty divided by fifteen, giving a mean square of two. Their ratio is twenty-one, the observed F statistic. The method row reports a p-value of approximately 0.00004484 using numerator and denominator degrees of freedom two and fifteen. The residual row supplies the denominator, not a separate hypothesis test here. Next, locate this ratio on its null reference distribution."@en ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-18-item-1 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-18-heading ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-18-item-2 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-18-table ; cd:communicativeRole cd:TableRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasTableRow" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-18-item-3 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-18-takeaway ; cd:communicativeRole cd:StatementRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-continuation-scene19 a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l7-next-19-focus ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l7-next-19-item-1, ex:l7-next-19-item-2, ex:l7-next-19-item-3 ; cd:narration "Now use the F distribution to give the observed ratio a scale. Its numerator degrees of freedom are two and its denominator degrees of freedom are fifteen. Locate the observed statistic at twenty-one, then follow the shaded area to its right. That area is approximately 0.00004484: under equal population means and the model assumptions, it is the probability of an F statistic at least this large. It is not the probability that the null hypothesis is true. Compare the area with the predefined 0.05 threshold, then examine whether the sampling and error assumptions justify that comparison."@en ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-19-item-1 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-19-heading ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-19-item-2 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-chart-19 ; cd:communicativeRole cd:ChartRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-19-item-3 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-19-takeaway ; cd:communicativeRole cd:StatementRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-scene20 a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l7-next-20-focus ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l7-next-20-item-1, ex:l7-next-20-item-2, ex:l7-next-20-item-3 ; cd:narration "Before accepting the inference, return to how the measurements were generated. Six independent preparations can provide six experimental units; six injections from one preparation do not become six independent preparations. Use the preparation diagram to check this distinction first. The classical model also assumes errors centered on zero, a common error variance across methods, and normally distributed errors for the exact F reference. Independence mainly comes from design knowledge, not a normality plot. Next, use residual diagnostics to investigate model departures that the observed measurements can reveal."@en ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-20-item-1 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-20-heading ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-20-item-2 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-20-cards ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DefinitionListRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasDefinitionListEntry" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-20-item-3 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-20-takeaway ; cd:communicativeRole cd:StatementRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-scene21 a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l7-next-21-focus ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l7-next-21-item-1, ex:l7-next-21-item-2, ex:l7-next-21-item-3 ; cd:narration "Having checked the design, inspect what residuals can tell us about model fit. A residual is an observed recovery minus its own method's sample mean; those means are the fitted values in this one-way model. On the residual-versus-fitted plot, compare vertical spread across the three fitted levels. A widening fan would suggest unequal variance. On the Q–Q plot, systematic departures from the reference line would question the normal error model. These plots use the original eighteen preparations; repeated residual values produce steps in the Q–Q plot. With six observations per method, neither a reassuring plot nor a normality test guarantees assumptions. Next, quantify effect size."@en ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-21-item-1 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-21-heading ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-21-item-2 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-21-visual ; cd:communicativeRole cd:MediaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:uri" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-21-item-3 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-21-takeaway ; cd:communicativeRole cd:StatementRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-scene22 a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l7-next-22-focus ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l7-next-22-item-1, ex:l7-next-22-item-2, ex:l7-next-22-item-3, ex:l7-next-22-item-4 ; cd:narration "Beyond the small p-value, ask how large the observed separation is. The variation graphic assigns eighty-four of 114 squared percentage points to differences between method means. Their ratio, eta squared, is approximately 0.737, or 73.7 percent of the observed total sum of squares. This describes the fitted sample decomposition, not a universal fraction of chemical variation or proof of causation. Also read the mean differences in their original scale: B minus A is four, C minus A is minus one, and C minus B is minus five percentage points. Compare these with application-specific recovery requirements before reporting."@en ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-22-item-1 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-22-heading ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-22-item-2 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-22-visual ; cd:communicativeRole cd:MediaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:uri" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-22-item-3 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-22-formula ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-22-item-4 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 4 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-22-takeaway ; cd:communicativeRole cd:StatementRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-scene23 a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l7-next-23-focus ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l7-next-23-item-1, ex:l7-next-23-item-2, ex:l7-next-23-item-3 ; cd:narration "Bring the calculation together in a report that preserves its limits. For these synthetic teaching data, a classical fixed-effects one-way ANOVA found evidence of a method effect on percent recovery, with F of twenty-one on two and fifteen degrees of freedom, p approximately 0.0000448, and eta squared 0.737. State that each method had six independent preparations and that inference depends on the sampling and error model. The global result supports at least one unequal population mean; it does not identify which pairs differ or establish practical superiority. Next, address the predefined comparisons with appropriate multiplicity control."@en ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-23-item-1 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-23-heading ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-23-item-2 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-23-cards ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DefinitionListRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasDefinitionListEntry" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-23-item-3 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-23-takeaway ; cd:communicativeRole cd:StatementRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-scene24 a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l7-next-24-focus ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l7-next-24-item-1, ex:l7-next-24-item-2, ex:l7-next-24-item-3 ; cd:narration "The overall ANOVA is significant, but that is the starting point for localization, not its conclusion. In this synthetic teaching example, the F statistic is 21, with 2 and 15 degrees of freedom. Keep the three unanswered comparisons in view: A versus B, A versus C, and B versus C. The global test tells us that the population means are not all equal under the model assumptions. It does not tell us whether A and C differ. Keep those pairwise questions separate as we next choose a rule for controlling errors across the comparison family."@en ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-24-item-1 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-24-heading ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-24-item-2 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-24-cards ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DefinitionListRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasDefinitionListEntry" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-24-item-3 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-24-takeaway ; cd:communicativeRole cd:StatementRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-scene25 a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l7-next-25-focus ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l7-next-25-item-1, ex:l7-next-25-item-2, ex:l7-next-25-item-3 ; cd:narration "Those three open questions belong to one comparison family. Treat them as one family for error control, even though their test statistics can be dependent. Family-wise error means the probability of making at least one false positive within that family. Testing each pair at an unadjusted five percent level does not generally keep the family risk at five percent. A significant omnibus F test alone is not a general license for unadjusted follow-ups. Define the family and strategy before inspecting results where possible. For all pairwise method comparisons here, we will use Tukey's approach."@en ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-25-item-1 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-25-heading ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-25-item-2 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-25-cards ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DefinitionListRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasDefinitionListEntry" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-25-item-3 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-25-takeaway ; cd:communicativeRole cd:StatementRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-scene26 a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l7-next-26-focus ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l7-next-26-item-1, ex:l7-next-26-item-2, ex:l7-next-26-item-3 ; cd:narration "With the comparison family defined, Tukey HSD puts every mean difference on the recovery scale. The synthetic example has six independent preparations per method and a pooled within-group mean square of 2 squared percentage points. Under the classical equal-variance ANOVA assumptions, Tukey combines that error estimate with the studentized-range critical value for three groups and fifteen error degrees of freedom. The resulting simultaneous interval half-width is 2.12083 percentage points. Notice the equal widths across the three contrast lines: that reflects this balanced design. Next, compare each interval with zero, rather than judging differences from the sample means alone."@en ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-26-item-1 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-26-heading ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-26-item-2 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-26-table ; cd:communicativeRole cd:TableRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasTableRow" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-26-item-3 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-26-takeaway ; cd:communicativeRole cd:StatementRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-scene27 a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l7-next-27-focus ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l7-next-27-item-1, ex:l7-next-27-item-2, ex:l7-next-27-item-3 ; cd:narration "Now read the forest plot against the vertical zero line. Each point is an observed sample mean difference; its interval represents model-based simultaneous uncertainty, not the spread of individual preparations. B minus A is positive four percentage points, with an interval entirely above zero. C minus B is negative five, entirely below zero. C minus A is negative one, and its interval crosses zero. Thus, this Tukey analysis identifies differences for the first and third comparisons, but not for A and C. Crossing zero is not proof of equality. Preserve each subtraction direction as we move to other follow-up strategies."@en ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-27-item-1 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-27-heading ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-27-item-2 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-27-visual ; cd:communicativeRole cd:MediaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:uri" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-27-item-3 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-27-takeaway ; cd:communicativeRole cd:StatementRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-scene28 a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l7-next-28-focus ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l7-next-28-item-1, ex:l7-next-28-item-2, ex:l7-next-28-item-3 ; cd:narration "The previous plot answered all pairwise questions under a common-variance model, but not every study asks that question. Read this decision guide as a schematic choice of analysis, not another result from the teaching data. Tukey suits all pairwise mean comparisons when its assumptions fit. For a defined family of planned contrasts, Holm or Bonferroni can provide multiplicity control using valid underlying tests. Properly specified planned contrasts can be tested without requiring a significant omnibus F first. With unequal variances, consider Games–Howell for pairwise comparisons, often alongside Welch's ANOVA. Next, ask whether a second explanatory factor changes the method comparison."@en ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-28-item-1 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-28-heading ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-28-item-2 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-28-cards ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DefinitionListRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasDefinitionListEntry" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-28-item-3 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-28-takeaway ; cd:communicativeRole cd:StatementRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-scene29 a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l7-next-29-focus ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l7-next-29-item-1, ex:l7-next-29-item-2, ex:l7-next-29-item-3 ; cd:narration "Until now, our comparison concerned river water alone. Look at the river water means: method A gives 94 percent recovery, B gives 98 percent, and C gives 93 percent. Now compare the wastewater means: A gives 92 percent, B gives 86 percent, and C gives 94 percent. These are synthetic teaching values, not experimental findings. Notice that B changes from the highest to the lowest mean. Before calling any method generally preferable, ask which matrix the conclusion describes. Next, we organize both factors in one crossed experimental design."@en ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-29-item-1 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-29-heading ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-29-item-2 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-29-visual ; cd:communicativeRole cd:MediaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:uri" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-29-item-3 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-29-takeaway ; cd:communicativeRole cd:StatementRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-scene30 a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l7-next-30-focus ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l7-next-30-item-1, ex:l7-next-30-item-2, ex:l7-next-30-item-3 ; cd:narration "To investigate this dependence, place the three methods in columns and the two matrices in rows. Each intersection is a distinct experimental condition, giving six cells altogether. In this balanced teaching design, every cell contains six independent sample preparations, for 36 observations in total. Count preparations, not repeated instrument readings of the same preparation, when checking this replication assumption. The grid represents the proposed experimental structure; it is not a display of individual measurements. A four by three design would contain twelve cells. Next, separate the average method effect from the average matrix effect."@en ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-30-item-1 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-30-heading ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-30-item-2 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-30-table ; cd:communicativeRole cd:TableRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasTableRow" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-30-item-3 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-30-takeaway ; cd:communicativeRole cd:StatementRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-scene31 a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l7-next-31-focus ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l7-next-31-item-1, ex:l7-next-31-item-2, ex:l7-next-31-item-3 ; cd:narration "With the design established, first average each method across the two matrices. Because the cell sizes are equal, river water and wastewater receive equal weight. For A, averaging 94 and 92 gives 93 percent recovery. For B, averaging 98 and 86 gives 92 percent. For C, averaging 93 and 94 gives 93.5 percent. Look at these marginal means rather than either matrix alone. B is no longer the method with the highest mean. These averages describe a main effect, but they conceal the contrasting matrix-specific pattern. Next, average in the other direction."@en ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-31-item-1 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-31-heading ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-31-item-2 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-31-table ; cd:communicativeRole cd:TableRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasTableRow" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-31-item-3 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-31-takeaway ; cd:communicativeRole cd:StatementRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-scene32 a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l7-next-32-focus ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l7-next-32-item-1, ex:l7-next-32-item-2, ex:l7-next-32-item-3 ; cd:narration "Now reverse the averaging direction and combine the three methods within each matrix. River water has mean recovery of 95.0 percent, while wastewater has mean recovery of approximately 90.667 percent. Look at the two displayed means: wastewater minus river water is approximately minus 4.333 percentage points. This is an average across methods, not a statement that every method loses the same amount. In fact, the individual method changes differ substantially. Use the matrix main effect to summarize the overall level, then return to the cell means to investigate how the method comparisons depend on matrix."@en ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-32-item-1 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-32-heading ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-32-item-2 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-32-table ; cd:communicativeRole cd:TableRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasTableRow" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-32-item-3 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-32-takeaway ; cd:communicativeRole cd:StatementRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-scene33 a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l7-next-33-focus ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l7-next-33-item-1, ex:l7-next-33-item-2, ex:l7-next-33-item-3 ; cd:narration "Return to the individual method lines, with matrix on the horizontal axis and recovery on the vertical axis. Read B minus A in river water: plus four percentage points. Read the same directed comparison in wastewater: minus six percentage points. Subtracting the river water difference from the wastewater difference gives minus ten percentage points. That changing comparison is an interaction pattern in these synthetic means. B itself drops twelve percentage points between matrices. The connecting lines guide comparisons; they do not describe change over time. Next, contrast this pattern with a hypothetical additive model."@en ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-33-item-1 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-33-heading ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-33-item-2 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-33-visual ; cd:communicativeRole cd:MediaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:uri" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-33-item-3 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-33-takeaway ; cd:communicativeRole cd:StatementRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-scene34 a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l7-next-34-focus ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l7-next-34-item-1, ex:l7-next-34-item-2, ex:l7-next-34-item-3 ; cd:narration "To clarify the distinction, compare the two panels using their matching scales. The additive panel is a hypothetical schematic: every method receives the same matrix shift, so the connecting lines are parallel. The other panel uses our supplied synthetic cell means, where the shifts differ by method. Focus on differences in slope, not line color or whether a crossing happens inside the plot. Nonparallel population mean profiles imply interaction even without a crossing; sample profiles alone still require statistical assessment. A main effect can also coexist with interaction. Next, add an explicit interaction term to the model."@en ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-34-item-1 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-34-heading ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-34-item-2 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-34-visual ; cd:communicativeRole cd:MediaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:uri" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-34-item-3 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-34-takeaway ; cd:communicativeRole cd:StatementRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-scene35 a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l7-next-35-focus ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l7-next-35-item-1, ex:l7-next-35-item-2, ex:l7-next-35-item-3, ex:l7-next-35-item-4 ; cd:narration "We can now express the design as a model for each preparation's recovery. Read the equation from left to right: the overall mean, the method contribution, the matrix contribution, their interaction, and a residual. The interaction describes departures from a purely additive combination, not another independent experimental factor. Match each index to the legend: method, matrix, and preparation within their cell. For the displayed parameterization, impose zero-sum constraints on main effects and on interaction rows and columns. Independent, normally distributed errors with common variance support the classical tests. Next, locate these components in the ANOVA table."@en ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-35-item-1 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-35-heading ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-35-item-2 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-35-cards ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DefinitionListRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasDefinitionListEntry" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-35-item-3 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-35-formula ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-35-item-4 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 4 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-35-takeaway ; cd:communicativeRole cd:StatementRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-scene36 a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l7-next-36-focus ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l7-next-36-item-1, ex:l7-next-36-item-2, ex:l7-next-36-item-3 ; cd:narration "Next, read each effect row against the same residual benchmark. The residual mean square is two, with thirty degrees of freedom. Method has an F statistic of 3.5 and a p value near 0.0430; matrix has 84.5 and approximately 3.13 times ten to the minus ten. Interaction has 69.5 and approximately 5.48 times ten to the minus twelve. All three tests meet the 0.05 threshold under the model assumptions. These are analyses of synthetic teaching data, not experimental findings. Inspect the interaction row before interpreting either marginal main effect as a general recommendation."@en ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-36-item-1 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-36-heading ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-36-item-2 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-36-table ; cd:communicativeRole cd:TableRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasTableRow" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-36-item-3 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-36-takeaway ; cd:communicativeRole cd:StatementRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-scene37 a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l7-next-37-focus ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l7-next-37-item-1, ex:l7-next-37-item-2, ex:l7-next-37-item-3 ; cd:narration "Given the interaction result, return to the two ends of the method profiles before making a recommendation. In river water, B minus A is plus four percentage points; in wastewater, B minus A is minus six. These are directed differences between synthetic cell means, not newly established pairwise significance results. To make inferential claims, estimate the relevant simple effects, meaning comparisons within a specified matrix, and use confidence intervals with a planned multiplicity strategy. Estimated marginal means can support those comparisons under the fitted model. Report the matrix context explicitly rather than naming one universal winner. Next, extend this reasoning to additional factors."@en ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-37-item-1 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-37-heading ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-37-item-2 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-37-visual ; cd:communicativeRole cd:MediaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:uri" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-37-item-3 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-37-takeaway ; cd:communicativeRole cd:StatementRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-scene38 a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l7-next-38-focus ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l7-next-38-item-1, ex:l7-next-38-item-2, ex:l7-next-38-item-3 ; cd:narration "Finally, add extraction temperature: three methods, two matrices, and two temperatures create twelve cells. Here A labels the method factor, B the matrix factor, and C temperature, not individual method names. A, B, and C are their averaged main effects. A:B means method comparisons depend on matrix; A:C means they depend on temperature; B:C means matrix comparisons depend on temperature. A:B:C means the method–matrix interaction itself depends on temperature. The table lists model terms, not measured temperature effects. Plan adequate independent replication and avoid missing combinations that prevent estimation. Next, distinguish adding factors from analyzing multiple response variables with MANOVA."@en ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-38-item-1 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-38-heading ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-38-item-2 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-38-table ; cd:communicativeRole cd:TableRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasTableRow" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-38-item-3 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-38-takeaway ; cd:communicativeRole cd:StatementRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-scene39 a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l7-next-39-focus ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l7-next-39-item-1, ex:l7-next-39-item-2, ex:l7-next-39-item-3 ; cd:narration "Before choosing an alternative, return to the experiment rather than starting with a normality test. Follow the decision path and identify the independent experimental unit first. Repeated measurements of one preparation do not automatically create independent replicates. Next, ask whether you have one response, several responses, or an entire chemical profile. For a single response, identify the factors and assess the error structure, including unequal variances. These branches describe choices, not observed results. Use the design to narrow the options before checking assumptions. Next, consider independent groups whose variances differ substantially."@en ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-39-item-1 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-39-heading ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-39-item-2 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-39-cards ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DefinitionListRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasDefinitionListEntry" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-39-item-3 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-39-takeaway ; cd:communicativeRole cd:StatementRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-scene40 a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l7-next-40-focus ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l7-next-40-item-1, ex:l7-next-40-item-2, ex:l7-next-40-item-3 ; cd:narration "With the design established, consider groups that have visibly different spreads. The clouds here are schematic: they illustrate unequal variability, not measured recoveries or a computed test result. Ordinary one-way ANOVA uses a common error variance, whereas Welch ANOVA accommodates different group variances through weighting and adjusted degrees of freedom. It still requires independent observations and does not automatically solve every distributional problem. If pairwise mean comparisons are needed, Games–Howell can be an appropriate follow-up. Check independence before selecting either procedure. Next, ask whether comparing ranks better matches the scientific question."@en ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-40-item-1 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-40-heading ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-40-item-2 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-40-visual ; cd:communicativeRole cd:MediaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:uri" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-40-item-3 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-40-takeaway ; cd:communicativeRole cd:StatementRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-scene41 a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l7-next-41-focus ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l7-next-41-item-1, ex:l7-next-41-item-2, ex:l7-next-41-item-3 ; cd:narration "A different question leads from means to ranks. Read the illustrative ranks across all groups together, not separately within each group. Equal recorded values receive average ranks, and the test requires a correction for ties. Kruskal–Wallis evaluates group differences through these pooled ranks; it is not universally a test of medians. A location or median interpretation needs additional assumptions about comparable distributional shapes. Check whether detection limits have produced censored observations rather than ordinary ties. Dunn comparisons with Holm adjustment may follow when appropriate. Next, distinguish multiple responses from multiple factors."@en ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-41-item-1 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-41-heading ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-41-item-2 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-41-table ; cd:communicativeRole cd:TableRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasTableRow" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-41-item-3 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-41-takeaway ; cd:communicativeRole cd:StatementRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-scene42 a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l7-next-42-focus ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l7-next-42-item-1, ex:l7-next-42-item-2, ex:l7-next-42-item-3 ; cd:narration "Now change the response structure rather than the number of factors. Each point in this schematic plot represents one independent preparation with recovery measurements for two analytes. Read both axes together and compare the marked group mean locations, remembering that no experimental outcome is established by this illustration. MANOVA asks whether the groups have different joint mean vectors while accounting for relationships between responses. Pillai's trace and Wilks' lambda are possible test summaries. Two responses do not make this a two-way ANOVA: factors and responses are different concepts. Next, extend the idea to entire chemical fingerprints."@en ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-42-item-1 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-42-heading ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-42-item-2 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-42-visual ; cd:communicativeRole cd:MediaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:uri" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-42-item-3 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-42-takeaway ; cd:communicativeRole cd:StatementRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-scene43 a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l7-next-43-focus ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l7-next-43-item-1, ex:l7-next-43-item-2, ex:l7-next-43-item-3 ; cd:narration "For a chemical fingerprint, the response is an entire profile rather than one recovery value. Follow the path from four peak areas per sample to distances between samples. Preprocessing and scaling must be chosen deliberately because they determine which peaks dominate those distances. Before using Bray–Curtis, check nonnegativity, the influence of abundant peaks, and whether normalization serves the scientific question. An ordination can illustrate the distances without replacing the test. PERMANOVA uses a pseudo-F statistic and design-appropriate permutations. Specify exchangeable units before testing. Next, examine why a significant result need not mean different group centers."@en ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-43-item-1 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-43-heading ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-43-item-2 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-43-diagram ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DiagramRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-43-item-3 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-43-takeaway ; cd:communicativeRole cd:StatementRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-scene44 a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l7-next-44-focus ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l7-next-44-item-1, ex:l7-next-44-item-2, ex:l7-next-44-item-3 ; cd:narration "The next distinction concerns what a distance-based result can mean. In the first schematic panel, compare group centers while noting their similar spread. In the second, compare spread around similar centers. Neither panel is experimental evidence; together they explain why a significant PERMANOVA does not by itself prove different centroids. Inspect the distance structure and assess multivariate dispersion, for example with betadisper and an appropriate permutation test. A dispersion check informs interpretation rather than automatically validating the main test. For paired samples, unrestricted permutations can break the pairing. Next, apply these design checks to three analytical cases."@en ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-44-item-1 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-44-heading ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-44-item-2 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-44-visual ; cd:communicativeRole cd:MediaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:uri" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-44-item-3 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-44-takeaway ; cd:communicativeRole cd:StatementRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-scene45 a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l7-next-45-focus ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l7-next-45-item-1, ex:l7-next-45-item-2, ex:l7-next-45-item-3 ; cd:narration "Apply the decision process before revealing a method name. For each case, identify the independent preparation, the response, and the factors. Three SPE methods with one recovery response suggest a one-way comparison, provided its assumptions fit. Three methods crossed with two sample matrices suggest a two-way model that includes their interaction: method differences may depend on matrix. Comparing LC-MS profiles can motivate PERMANOVA after preprocessing, distance selection, and permutation restrictions are specified. These are candidate analyses, not results. Discuss each choice before the instructor reveals it, using keyboard-accessible controls. Next, interpret an output without relying on significance stars."@en ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-45-item-1 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-45-heading ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-45-item-2 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-45-diagram ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DiagramRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-45-item-3 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-45-takeaway ; cd:communicativeRole cd:StatementRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-scene46 a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l7-next-46-focus ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l7-next-46-item-1, ex:l7-next-46-item-2, ex:l7-next-46-item-3 ; cd:narration "Now read the verified synthetic teaching output as evidence within a specified model. The balanced one-way example uses six independent preparations per method and reports F of 21 with 2 and 15 degrees of freedom, p approximately 0.0000448, and eta squared approximately 0.737. Identify this as evidence against equal method means, not proof that every pair differs. Ask for diagnostics and adjusted pairwise comparisons before naming differences. Separately, the two-way example has a significant method-by-matrix interaction, so marginal method effects are not within-matrix conclusions. Next, connect these reporting decisions to explained and unexplained variation."@en ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-46-item-1 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-46-heading ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-46-item-2 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-46-table ; cd:communicativeRole cd:TableRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasTableRow" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-46-item-3 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-46-takeaway ; cd:communicativeRole cd:StatementRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-scene47 a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l7-next-47-focus ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l7-next-47-item-1, ex:l7-next-47-item-2, ex:l7-next-47-item-3 ; cd:narration "Finally, follow the pathway from the experiment to the report. Factors define the comparisons, fitted group or cell means represent the model, and sums of squares organize variation. Dividing those sums by their degrees of freedom gives mean squares, making differently sized sources comparable. Under the relevant null hypothesis and model assumptions, the F ratio compares a tested source with the appropriate error estimate. A large ratio prompts statistical evaluation, not an automatic scientific conclusion. Check diagnostics, quantify effects, and choose justified follow-up comparisons. This same modeling perspective leads onward to regression, factorial designs, and mixed models."@en ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-47-item-1 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-47-heading ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-47-item-2 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-47-diagram ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DiagramRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l7-next-47-item-3 a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l7-next-47-takeaway ; cd:communicativeRole cd:StatementRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:hasSource ex:l7-source-package ; cd:authoredResource true .
